extends Node
class_name BossDatabase
## Per-colour boss identity: which model it wears, what its telegraphed specials do, and
## how the fight changes as it loses health.
##
## Each of the five MTG colours gets one boss. The visual scenes are generated by
## tools/boss_character_builder.gd and are all normalized to 1.7 units tall, so
## `model_scale` here is the single knob controlling how physically large a boss
## is - and, through GameSettings.get_boss_anim_speed(), how slowly it animates.
##
## A spawned boss can additionally carry a named, MTG-flavoured MODIFIER (Riot,
## Annihilator, Cataclysm, Bloodthirst, Enrage, Lifelink) that reshapes how the
## SPECIALS below are used, without ever touching this Dictionary itself - see
## GameSettings' Boss modifiers block for every number and EnemyBase.apply_boss_modifier /
## _begin_special for how they're applied to a DUPLICATE of a special's config. This
## Dictionary is shared by every boss of a colour across every match; nothing may mutate it
## in place.
##
## Every boss fights in THREE PHASES (GameSettings' Boss phases block holds the health
## thresholds). Each colour is built to feel different, and the phases are where that lives:
##
##   Red    pressure and tempo       - never lets you stand still, fills the ground with fire
##   Blue   control of space         - slow and readable, but slows you and takes ground away
##   Green  unstoppable weight       - longest tells, biggest hits, brings the forest with it
##   White  discipline and duelling  - lines and crosses, a shield you have to walk around
##   Black  attrition and the hunt   - gets stronger the longer it lasts, hunts the weakest
##
## Phase 2 gives every boss an answer to being kited (something that reaches a player who
## stands off), and phase 3 turns an attack the player already knows into a new one.

## Boss visual scenes are looked up by path rather than preload()ed so a missing
## or not-yet-built scene degrades to the melee fallback in EnemyBase instead of
## failing to parse this script and taking the whole project down with it.
const VISUAL_SCENES := {
	"Red": "res://scenes/bosses/fire_giant.tscn",
	"Blue": "res://scenes/bosses/frost_giant.tscn",
	"Green": "res://scenes/bosses/treant.tscn",
	"White": "res://scenes/bosses/white_paladin.tscn",
	"Black": "res://scenes/bosses/zombie_lord.tscn",
}

## Bigger = slower animations (see GameSettings.boss_anim_reference_scale, which
## is set to the smallest of these so no boss ends up animating faster than 1.0x).
const MODEL_SCALES := {
	"Red": 2.4,
	"Blue": 2.6,
	"Green": 2.9,
	"White": 2.1,
	"Black": 2.3,
}

## Telegraphed, dodgeable special attacks - between them the boss's whole offence. Bosses
## have no ordinary swing at all.
##
## The plain `attack` clip used to drive an undodgeable melee hit on a 1.8s timer, which is the
## one thing in the game a player could not answer: stand in front of a boss and you simply
## took it. Now that clip drives a short-range special - short cooldown, still telegraphed - so
## every hit can be walked out of. What a boss does is entirely made of things the player is
## given a tell for.
##
## Index 0 is always the big phase-1 area attack and index 1 the melee one (the only entry
## flagged hits_crystal). Later entries are the phase specials.
##
## Per-special keys - every one optional except where the shape needs it:
##   clip             which animation drives it ("special", "attack" or "hit")
##   phases           which phases it is used in. Default: all three
##   priority         considered before lower ones when several are ready. Default 0; entries
##                    of equal priority keep their order here, so the big one still wins
##                    wherever it and the melee one are both legal
##   min_range        held back below this distance to the target
##   max_range        and never started from further than this. Default: what the shape reaches
##   cooldown         its own. Default GameSettings.boss_special_cooldown
##   cooldown_by_phase  {phase: seconds}, overriding `cooldown` from that phase on
##   hits_crystal     whether it damages the objective. TRUE only for the melee special: the
##                    crystal cannot dodge, so giving anything else a free hit on it would be
##                    undodgeable damage by another name
##   impact_fraction  where in the clip the hit lands, as a fraction of the clip's own length.
##                    Expressed that way rather than in seconds so the windup stretches for
##                    bigger bosses (whose clips play slower) and the tell stays readable
##   windup           a FIXED tell in seconds instead, for attacks aimed somewhere other than
##                    at the boss's own feet, whose tell must not depend on the clip
##   shape            circle | cone | ring | line | cross
##                      circle  `radius` around its centre
##                      cone    an `angle`-degree arc of `radius` along the facing - dodged by
##                              getting behind it
##                      ring    between `inner_radius` and `radius` - the middle is SAFE
##                      line    `length` x `width` along the facing; `lines` fans several out
##                              at those yaw offsets (degrees)
##                      cross   two lines through the centre at right angles
##   target           who it is aimed at:
##                      current          whoever the boss is already fighting (the default)
##                      nearest_player / farthest_player / weakest_player / random_player
##                      each_player      one strike under every player in range at once
##   anchor           self (centred on the boss, the default) | target (centred where the
##                    target stands) | follow (rides on the boss while it moves)
##   extra_random     each_player only: this many more strikes near a random player
##   move             charge (dashes along its own line as it lands) | leap (travels to the
##                    target over the windup) | follow (walks at the target throughout)
##   slow             seconds of slow on every player it hits
##   hazard           ground it leaves at every centre it struck - see BossHazard
##   hazard_from_phase  only leaves it from this phase on
##   followups        further strikes chained after this one lands, each its own small
##                    special: `delay` after the previous hit, its own `windup`, and an
##                    `anchor` of same (the previous strike's centres), self, follow, or
##                    retarget (turn to a fresh `target` first)
##   exhaust          {time, mult}: after the whole thing ends the boss stands open for
##                    `time` seconds and takes `mult` times damage. The punish window.
##   kind             raise_dead | consecrate - the two that are not a hit at all
##   tell             a tell in the world beside the ground telegraph, for attacks the boss's
##                    body cannot announce on its own - see BossTell. Shown even with
##                    the ground telegraphs switched off
